### A Year of Recalls: Some Brands Just Don't Learn

With 500 food recalls so far this year, it's clear that the industry isn't getting any safer. Among these, 144 are classified as Class I recalls, indicating a high risk of serious adverse health consequences or death. The culprit behind 152 of these recalls? Our old nemesis: Salmonella. That's a 45% share of Class I recalls caused by one pathogen.

### Repeat Offenders: The Usual Suspects

Surpassing even the most pessimistic expectations, the industry saw 82 repeat offenders this year alone. Moonlight Packing Corporation leads the dubious honor roll with an 'F' grade, amassing 11 recalls, all of which are Class I. They're joined by Ghirardelli Chocolate Company and Albertsons Companies LLC, each with 10 Class I recalls. One has to wonder: how many warnings does it take before systemic changes are implemented?

### A Particularly Busy Month

May 2026 was particularly grim, with 173 recalls, the highest monthly count in the past year. Contributing to this spike are big names like Wellnov Supplements LLC (14 recalls this year alone) and Ghirardelli Chocolate Company, recently involved in two separate Salmonella contamination cases for their Sweet Ground Powder and Frappe Mix. Both products were distributed nationwide, broadening the scope of potential impact.

### Spotlight on Safety: The Latest Offenses

Recent recalls include "Ellsworth Sour Cream + Onion Seasoning" and "Natural Cream of Mushroom Type Flavor," both pulled due to Salmonella contamination originating from the same milk powder supplier. Meanwhile, Liquid Blenz Corp faced a Class II recall for their Menopause Bully drink, which might promote botulism growth--an issue that could be deadly. These serve as stark reminders of the cascading failures in the supply chain when upstream suppliers make mistakes.

### The Accountability Question

The troubling frequency of these incidents points to a need for more stringent oversight. It's clear that reliance on self-regulation is falling short. While mistakes happen, the frequency and severity of food safety violations in the industry indicate systemic issues. How many Class I recalls does it take before companies either shape up or ship out?

### Shelf Check Recommendation

Consumers should remain vigilant. Check the FDA website regularly for recall announcements, and always be cautious with food items during recall-heavy periods, especially when they involve major brands or bulk suppliers. Food safety is a shared responsibility--but the onus is primarily on manufacturers and regulators to ensure the food we consume doesn't harm us.
